{{Short description|Stylistic device characteristic of LLMs}}
{{One source|date=July 2026}}
'''Negative parallelism''' is a [[stylistic device]] that defines a subject by describing what it is not as well as what it is: "it's not X, it's Y".<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Oremus |first=Will |date=2026-07-12 |title=The Most Famous AI Writing Tic Is Also the Most Mysterious |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/2026/07/ai-chatbot-writing-tic-negative-parallelism/687892/ |access-date=2026-07-13 |website=[[The Atlantic]] |language=en}}</ref> It has also been called "[[antithesis]]", "metalinguistic negation" or "contrastive phrasing".<ref name=":0" />

The device has been employed by writers such as [[William Shakespeare|Shakespeare]] ("The fault, dear Brutus, is not in our stars, but in ourselves"; ''[[Julius Caesar (play)|Julius Caesar]])'' or by the football coach [[Vince Lombardi]] ("Winning isn’t everything; it’s the only thing").<ref name=":0" /> As a distinct element of style, however, it gained attention only in the 2020s when it became apparent that [[Large language model|large language models]] (LLMs) such as ChatGPT use negative parallelism inordinately often (about three times more frequently than humans), to the point that such constructions came to be seen as a hallmark of machine-generated text.<ref name=":0" />

It is not clear why LLMs are so fond of negative parallelism. It might just be because such constructions are frequent in training data.<ref name=":0" /> Another possible explanation is that during [[Reinforcement learning from human feedback|reinforcement learning]], a phase in LLM training, human reviewers might grade negative parallelism higher because it conveys "the impression of nuance and insight".<ref name=":0" /> Finally, negative parallelism could be an artefact of the way LLMs generate text. Fundamentally, they do so by "next-token prediction", choosing words in part based on how statistically likely it is that they follow the previous word, as well as how likely it is that they result in a highly rated response. Negative parallelism could be a way for an LLM to attempt to satisfy both incentives by choosing a relatively easy to find (and therefore likely) negative descriptor as well as a more interesting (and therefore highly rated) positive descriptor.<ref name=":0" /> 

Because LLMs are increasingly trained by and based on the output of other LLMs, tics such as a preference for negative parallelisms tend to be self-reinforcing and therefore hard to root out.<ref name=":0" /> While this might be an advantage for those who wish to distinguish artificial from human-written text, there are indications that the prevalence of negative parallelism in LLM-generated text – which is an increasing proportion of all text – might in turn be influencing human conversations, making humans more likely to adopt the style of LLMs.<ref name=":0" />

